  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「エダウチオオバコ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%A8%E3%83%80%E3%82%A6%E3%83%81%E3%82%AA%E3%82%AA%E3%83%90%E3%82%B3",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106858321,
      "text": "エダウチオオバコ は、オオバコ科オオバコ属の一年草。ヨーロッパから西アジア地域原産の一年草。花期は晩春から初夏。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Plantago indica」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plantago_indica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1318684464,
      "text": "Plantago indica, commonly known as branched plantain, sand plantain, or black psyllium, is a flowering plant in the plantain family Plantaginaceae, and is one of a group of species in the genus Plantago formerly treated by some authors in a separate genus Psyllium. The plant is native to parts of Africa, Europe, Russia, and Asia, and is naturalised in many other areas such as Australia and North America. The plant can be found mostly in dry inland areas, such as those that are sandy, and also grows on roadsides and in meadows. The plant is not used broadly as a food source, but has been cultivated for its seeds which serve a medicinal use as a laxative."
    }
